{% comment %}
A "template" block can't nest inside another one — TRMNL's TemplateTag closes on the first
literal closing token with no nesting awareness. So badge_circle/chip_body/chip are top-level
siblings of "main", rendered from inside its body instead.
NOTE: this comment never spells out the actual curly-brace-percent tag delimiters as literal
text — Liquid tokenizes the whole file on those delimiters before any tag's own semantics run,
so writing them out here would itself parse as a malformed tag and corrupt the rest of the file.
{% endcomment %}

{% template badge_circle %}
  {%- comment -%} One small circle badge — used only by the header's people gutter now
  (category icons, "image" display mode, and photo badges were all removed together — see
  chip's own comment below). Always a person's badge letter; color is a param since the header
  colors each one per-person. Params: b, size (px), border (px), hue, fg, extra (extra
  class(es), e.g. inter-badge gap). {%- endcomment -%}
  <span class="rounded--small w--[{{ size }}px] h--[{{ size }}px] shrink-0 flex flex--center-x flex--center-y text--{{ fg }} bg--{{ hue }} {{ extra }}" style="overflow:hidden;box-sizing:border-box;border:{{ border }}px solid currentColor;">
    {{- b.text -}}
  </span>
{% endtemplate %}

{% template chip_body %}
  {%- comment -%} Title only, centered both axes — no category icon in this rail any more.
  Icons used to float top-left here with the title wrapping around them, but competing with
  the title for width AND height produced erratic font sizes (a short title stuck small next
  to an icon while a longer, icon-free title elsewhere rendered huge) — removed so fit() can
  scale purely on the box's own room. Still flex-centered rather than plain block flow so a
  single-line title actually sits in the middle of a tall box instead of pinned to the top.
  min-width:0 on both the flex container and the title keeps the title free to shrink/wrap
  instead of a flex child's default min-width:auto refusing to narrow past its content size.
  Params: title, fg, title_cls, fit_min, clip, pad_right.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="w--full h--full flex flex--row flex--center-x flex--center-y" style="min-width:0;">
    <span class="title {{ title_cls }} text--{{ fg }} cal-fit-text{% if clip %} cal-fit-clip{% endif %}" data-fit-min="{{ fit_min }}" data-fit-max="90" style="padding-right:{{ pad_right }}px;min-width:0;text-align:center;">{{ title }}</span>
  </div>
{% endtemplate %}

{% template chip %}
  {%- comment -%}
  The whole event/all-day chip box — fill, border, radius, and (via chip_body) its content —
  one render call for both chip types instead of two hand-kept-in-sync copies.
  Params only cover what's structurally forced to differ:
    - size_class/size_style: an all-day chip is normal-flow and needs an explicit h--[Ncqh] +
      inline height/flex-shrink; a timed event is sized by its absolutely-positioned ancestor,
      so it just fills that with h--full/w--full.
    - flex_dir: flex--row vs flex--col — cosmetic only, kept as whatever each caller already had.
    - pad_cls: all-day rows are short/fixed (py--[1px]); a timed event uses its view tier's own
      pt/pb (event_pad_cls in "main").
    - box_radius/border_left/border_right/margin_left/margin_right: only an all-day chip can be
      one fragment of a multi-day banner needing a squared-off mid-span edge; a timed event never
      spans day columns, so it always gets the full radius and every border side.
  Every chip is title-only now (chip_body) — category icons, "image" display mode, and photo
  badges were all removed together (categories were the only thing that ever set any of them).
  Params: hue, fg, title, flex_dir, size_class, size_style, pad_cls, box_radius, border_left,
  border_right, margin_left, margin_right, title_cls, fit_min, clip, pad_right.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="{{ size_class }} bg--{{ hue }} text--{{ fg }} w--full {{ pad_cls }} flex {{ flex_dir }} flex--center-y cal-fit-box relative" style="overflow:hidden;box-sizing:border-box;{{ size_style }}border-radius:{{ box_radius }};border-top:2px solid currentColor;border-bottom:2px solid currentColor;{% if border_left %}border-left:2px solid currentColor;{% endif %}{% if border_right %}border-right:2px solid currentColor;{% endif %}{% if margin_left %}margin-left:-4px;{% endif %}{% if margin_right %}margin-right:-4px;{% endif %}">
    {%- render "chip_body", title: title, fg: fg, title_cls: title_cls, fit_min: fit_min, clip: clip, pad_right: pad_right -%}
  </div>
{% endtemplate %}

{% template main %}
<style>
/* The header's people-badge gutter is the same narrow col--span-1 column the hour axis sits in
   — comfortably wide in landscape (full view's own 800px), but rotated to portrait the SAME
   device is only ~480px wide, too narrow for a 2-column badge grid at full view's landscape
   badge size (24px). Clamp back down only there — no plain utility class covers "different
   fixed pixel size on this one responsive combo" for an arbitrary w--[Npx]/h--[Npx] pair. */
.trmnl .screen--lg.screen--portrait .cal-head-badge { width: 16px !important; height: 16px !important; }
/* font-size set in run() (a fraction of the sibling hour span's real computed size — see the
   hour-row markup below); only the static, data-independent part lives in this rule. */
.cal-axis-period { color: #999; font-weight: normal; white-space: nowrap; margin-right: 2px; }
</style>
{%- if view == 4 -%}
  {%- comment -%} quadrant: a quarter of the full-view screen area — its own, smaller floor.
  event_title_cls gets a real static tier (not just the bare 26px "title" default) so a title
  still reads sanely if the fit-text JS doesn't run in time on whatever renders the screenshot. {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign head_title_cls = "title--small" -%}
  {%- assign chip_title_cls = "title--small" -%}
  {%- assign axis_cls = "label--small" -%}
  {%- assign event_pad_cls = "pt--0 pb--1" -%}
  {%- assign event_title_cls = "title--small" -%}
  {%- assign pad_right = 4 -%}
  {%- assign head_badge_d = 11 -%}
{%- elsif view == 3 -%}
  {%- comment -%} half_vertical: only ~400px WIDE (narrow day columns) — width-constrained
  rather than area-constrained, so fonts follow quadrant's tighter tier even though the screen
  is full-height. head_badge_d is its own case: the header gutter's WIDTH is the same 1/13
  share of this view's narrow 400px (so still width-bound), but its HEIGHT is the full-height
  view's own tall header row (unlike quadrant/half_horizontal's short one) — so it lands on
  its own tier, not simply quadrant's. {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign head_title_cls = "title--small" -%}
  {%- assign chip_title_cls = "title--small" -%}
  {%- assign axis_cls = "label--base lg:label--large" -%}
  {%- assign event_pad_cls = "pt--0 pb--1" -%}
  {%- assign event_title_cls = "title--small" -%}
  {%- assign pad_right = 4 -%}
  {%- assign head_badge_d = 13 -%}
{%- elsif view == 2 -%}
  {%- comment -%} half_horizontal: full 800px width but half screen HEIGHT (~240px, same as
  quadrant) — width is ample so chip_title_cls can run bigger, but head_badge_d shares
  quadrant's short tier since the gutter's HEIGHT (not width — it's still 800px wide here) is
  the same short header_pct row height both views are bound by. {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign head_title_cls = "title--small" -%}
  {%- assign chip_title_cls = "title--small lg:title--base" -%}
  {%- assign axis_cls = "label--base lg:label--large" -%}
  {%- assign event_pad_cls = "pt--1 pb--2" -%}
  {%- assign event_title_cls = "title--small" -%}
  {%- assign pad_right = 8 -%}
  {%- assign head_badge_d = 11 -%}
{%- else -%}
  {%- comment -%} full / "OG" 800x480 device: the hour axis reads too big at xlarge/xxlarge in
  practice, so one tier down. Rotated to portrait the device has taller rows/narrower columns, so
  temps and the day label both get an extra tier there (lg:portrait: is a real generated
  combinator). head_title_cls used to escalate all the way to lg:title--xxlarge (40px) on plain
  landscape too — confirmed on a real screen--lg device (which `trmnlp build`'s local preview
  can't simulate, so this only showed up once actually deployed) as badly oversized against an
  11%-tall header row; capped at title--large instead. {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign head_title_cls = "title--large lg:title--large lg:portrait:title--xlarge" -%}
  {%- assign chip_title_cls = "title--base lg:title--large lg:portrait:title--xlarge" -%}
  {%- assign axis_cls = "label--large lg:label--xxlarge" -%}
  {%- assign event_pad_cls = "pt--1 pb--2" -%}
  {%- assign event_title_cls = "title--base" -%}
  {%- assign pad_right = 8 -%}
  {%- assign head_badge_d = 24 -%}
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%} No category icons, photo badges, or "image" display mode anywhere any more —
every timed event chip and all-day bar is title-only (chip_body). pad_right is the title's
fixed breathing room so it isn't flush against the box edge.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- comment -%} The "OG" 800x480 full view is the only one wide enough for multiple day
columns to stay legible — every other view (quadrant/half_horizontal/half_vertical) always
shows just today, regardless of the Days to Show setting. `days` itself (transform.js's
output) is shared across all 4 view templates unchanged — transform.js has no idea which
view will actually render it, so this is a render-time restriction here, not a data one:
everything below reads shown_days_n (this view's real visible day count) instead of
days.size (the setting's own, possibly larger, day count) wherever a column count or
column-index math is involved. {%- endcomment -%}
{%- if view == 1 -%}
  {%- assign shown_days_n = days.size -%}
{%- else -%}
  {%- assign shown_days_n = 1 -%}
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%} Every tier above assumes the original 3-day layout's column width — force the
day-label down a tier once there are more columns than that (5-7 day views get much narrower
columns), regardless of view. {%- endcomment -%}
{%- if shown_days_n >= 6 -%}
  {%- assign head_title_cls = "title--small" -%}
  {%- assign chip_title_cls = "title--small" -%}
{%- elsif shown_days_n >= 4 -%}
  {%- assign head_title_cls = "title--base lg:title--large" -%}
  {%- assign chip_title_cls = "title--small" -%}
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%} The hour-axis column is a fixed col--span-1 against each day column's
col--span-4; the framework's grid auto-sizes tracks from the SUM of spans present in the row, so
the axis's width share is 1/(1 + 4*shown_days_n) — fine at the 3-day baseline this was tuned
against, but a wildly disproportionate ~20% at 1 day. Scale each day column's own span up as
shown_days_n drops below 3 so the total stays pinned at 13 (the 3-day baseline). {%- endcomment -%}
{%- if shown_days_n == 1 -%}
  {%- assign day_span = 12 -%}
{%- elsif shown_days_n == 2 -%}
  {%- assign day_span = 6 -%}
{%- else -%}
  {%- assign day_span = 4 -%}
{%- endif -%}

<div class="layout layout--col gap--none" id="cal-root">
  {% if error and has_events == false %}
    <!-- Only ever shown when EVERY calendar failed to fetch (has_events false) — not per-feed
         partial-failure noise, which just quietly renders whatever did come back instead (see
         run() in transform.js). Deliberately its own full-screen state, not the bare
         item/content/description list-row markup this used to reuse: that combo is meant for a
         compact list ENTRY (line-height:0, flex-end-justified — confirmed against the
         framework's own CSS), not a lone full-screen message, and rendered cramped/unstyled
         here. A calendar-with-an-x icon reads at a glance even before the text does; the raw
         error text is real diagnostic detail (e.g. "HTTP 404"), so it stays legible but
         visually secondary (smaller, gray) under the plain-language headline, clamped so a
         long one can't run off this small a screen. -->
    <div class="flex flex--col flex--center-x flex--center-y w--full h--full" style="box-sizing:border-box;padding:24px;gap:12px;text-align:center;">
      <img src="https://fonts.gstatic.com/s/i/short-term/release/materialsymbolsoutlined/event_busy/default/48px.svg" alt="" style="width:15%;max-width:56px;height:auto;">
      <span class="title title--large">{{ unavailable_label }}</span>
      <span class="description text--gray-50" data-clamp="3" style="max-width:85%;">{{ error }}</span>
    </div>
  {% else %}
    <!-- Native framework grid, not an image: percentages (h--[Ncqh]) are computed server-side
         in transform.js as a share of the whole screen, so this fills any device — including
         the larger, portrait, 4-bit TRMNL X — via the framework's own container-query system. -->

    <!-- Header: its own solid-black zone, day label only (weather moved to its own footer zone
         below the grid — see footer_pct). flex-shrink:0/flex-grow:0 + a duplicated inline
         height alongside the h--[Ncqh] class: a flex child of #cal-root's flex--col can get
         resized by its container without this, same defensive fix used on every fixed-size
         zone in this file. -->
    <div class="grid gap--none h--[{{ header_pct }}cqh] bg--black shrink-0" style="flex-grow:0;height:{{ header_pct }}cqh;min-height:{{ header_pct }}cqh;overflow:hidden;">
      <!-- Top-left gutter cell — everyone with anything anywhere in the whole visible range
           (run()'s viewPeople) gets one small badge here: one shared spot instead of a
           duplicate badge row under every day, which used to force the header taller on some
           renders than others and misalign one day's own label against the rest. -->
      <div class="flex flex--col flex--center-x flex--center-y" style="overflow:hidden;box-sizing:border-box;padding:1px;">
        {%- comment -%} Person badges are full-view only — the smaller/narrower views
        (quadrant/half_horizontal/half_vertical) don't have room to spare in this gutter once
        it's also carrying the hour axis below it (see head_badge_d's own per-view tiers
        above); the badge grid stays reserved for the one view wide/tall enough for it. {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- if people.size > 0 and view == 1 -%}
          <!-- justify-content:center overrides the base .grid utility's own width:100% (it
               forces the grid to fill the flex parent's cross axis, which otherwise defeats
               the parent's flex--center-x — the fixed-width columns below then sat flush left
               with dead space to their right instead of centered as a block). -->

          <div class="grid gap--[1px]" style="grid-template-columns:repeat(2, {{ head_badge_d }}px);justify-content:center;">
            {%- for b in people -%}
              {%- render "badge_circle", b: b, size: head_badge_d, border: 1, hue: b.hue, fg: b.fg, extra: "cal-head-badge" -%}
            {%- endfor -%}
          </div>
        {%- endif -%}
      </div>
      {% for day in days limit: shown_days_n %}
        <!-- flex--center-y: the gutter cell beside these can be taller than a bare label line
             once it's hosting a badge grid, so this keeps the date line centered against the
             header's own full height rather than pinned to its top edge. -->
        <div class="col--span-{{ day_span }} flex flex--col flex--center-x flex--center-y border--v-1">
          <!-- min-width:0 undoes the flex default of min-width:auto, which otherwise defeats
               overflow truncation on the child — needed at 5-7 day columns or quadrant's
               narrower portrait slot, where the label can genuinely run out of room. -->
          <div class="rounded--small px--[10px] py--[2px] w--max-full flex flex--col flex--center-x gap--[2px]" style="min-width:0;overflow:hidden;">
            <!-- Weekday abbreviation muted gray, day+month bold white — kept as two spans (not
                 one joined string) so they can carry different styling. Uppercased via CSS, not
                 by upper-casing the source text, so this stays purely a display choice.
                 color:white/#999 inline, not text--white: .title sets its own explicit color
                 (not currentColor), so it wouldn't inherit an ancestor's. data-clamp hid this
                 span outright instead of truncating it (framework Clamp engine didn't like the
                 nested-span/inline-color markup here) — back to plain ellipsis CSS. -->
            <span class="title {{ head_title_cls }}" style="white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;display:block;min-width:0;text-transform:uppercase;"><span style="color:#999;">{{ day.label_short_weekday }}</span> <span style="color:white;font-weight:bold;">{{ day.label_short_rest }}</span></span>
          </div>
        </div>
      {% endfor %}
    </div>

    {% if allday_pct > 0 %}
      {%- comment -%} A multi-day all-day event used to repeat as an identical, fully-rounded
      pill in every day column it touched, reading as several unrelated events instead of one.
      allday_bars (see layoutNative in transform.js) is a flat list — each bar already knows its
      own real start_col/span/row (a bar spanning several days is one array entry, not one per
      day) — placed with an EXPLICIT grid-column-start computed from start_col, rather than this
      file's usual auto-placement-infers-the-column-count trick (see the day-column comment
      above): that trick only ever needed to work for one auto-placed row of items, and explicit
      placement needs the total column count stated up front anyway (below), so there's nothing
      left for it to infer here. Explicit placement also means gaps between bars need no filler
      cells at all — an unoccupied explicit grid cell is just blank space.
      NOTE for next time this goes quiet locally: `{% render "main", ... %}` (in each view's own
      full.liquid/quadrant.liquid/etc) is a real Liquid `render`, which — unlike `include` —
      never inherits the outer scope; a variable only exists inside "main" if it's listed as a
      named param THERE. allday_bars/allday_max_rows both being newly-added top-level output
      fields, they silently read as empty inside "main" until also added to that render-param
      list in all four view files (not just returned from transform.js/added to .trmnlp.yml) —
      lost real time to this reading like a trmnlp mock-data limitation before it clicked.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- assign allday_total_units = day_span | times: shown_days_n | plus: 1 -%}
      <div class="grid gap--none h--[{{ allday_pct }}cqh] shrink-0 relative" style="grid-template-columns:repeat({{ allday_total_units }}, 1fr);flex-grow:0;height:{{ allday_pct }}cqh;min-height:{{ allday_pct }}cqh;overflow:hidden;">
        <!-- Gutter marker for the all-day band, same column the hour axis sits in below — a
             fixed-size glyph instead of all_day_label's own translated word (see allDayText in
             transform.js), so it fits this narrow column regardless of locale. all_day_label
             still comes through as alt text for anything that reads it. Spans every stacking
             row (allday_max_rows) the band actually has, so it stays one single icon for the
             whole band regardless of how many rows that turns out to be. -->
        <div class="col--span-1 flex flex--col flex--center-x flex--center-y" style="grid-row:1 / span {{ allday_max_rows }};overflow:hidden;box-sizing:border-box;padding:0 2px;">
          <img src="https://fonts.gstatic.com/s/i/short-term/release/materialsymbolsoutlined/today/default/24px.svg" alt="{{ all_day_label }}" style="width:85%;max-width:24px;height:auto;">
        </div>
        {% for bar in allday_bars %}
          {% if bar.start_col < shown_days_n %}
          <!-- A bar's start_col/span are relative to the FULL days.size window (transform.js has
               no idea a non-full view is about to clip that down to shown_days_n) — a bar
               starting beyond what this view actually shows is skipped entirely (the if above),
               and one that starts within it but would normally span past it gets its rendered
               span clamped to what's left, forced into the "continues_after" squared-off edge
               regardless of its own real continues_after (true or not, it now visibly runs off
               the edge of what this view shows, same visual promise either way). -->
          {% assign allday_visible_span = shown_days_n | minus: bar.start_col %}
          {% assign ad_continues_after = bar.continues_after %}
          {% if bar.span > allday_visible_span %}
            {% assign ad_continues_after = true %}
          {% else %}
            {% assign allday_visible_span = bar.span %}
          {% endif %}
          <!-- Square off whichever edge extends past the visible window (continues_before/after
               are relative to the WHOLE window now, not one day's own boundary) — a hint that
               this keeps going, rather than a fully-rounded pill implying it starts/ends right
               there. No more per-fragment border/margin bleeding: this is really one element
               now, nothing to visually stitch back together. -->
          {% if bar.continues_before and ad_continues_after %}
            {% assign ad_radius = "0px" %}
          {% elsif ad_continues_after %}
            {% assign ad_radius = "7px 0 0 7px" %}
          {% elsif bar.continues_before %}
            {% assign ad_radius = "0 7px 7px 0" %}
          {% else %}
            {% assign ad_radius = "7px" %}
          {% endif %}
          <!-- Column 1 is the gutter; day 0 starts at line 2, day N at line 2 + day_span*N. -->
          {% assign allday_col_start = day_span | times: bar.start_col | plus: 2 %}
          {% assign allday_bar_span = day_span | times: allday_visible_span %}
          {% assign allday_row_num = bar.row | plus: 1 %}
          <div class="bg--{{ bar.hue }} text--{{ bar.fg }} flex flex--row flex--center-x flex--center-y cal-fit-box" style="grid-column:{{ allday_col_start }} / span {{ allday_bar_span }};grid-row:{{ allday_row_num }};margin:0 1px;overflow:hidden;box-sizing:border-box;border-radius:{{ ad_radius }};border:2px solid currentColor;">
            <span class="title {{ chip_title_cls }} text--{{ bar.fg }} cal-fit-text cal-fit-clip" data-fit-min="9" data-fit-max="90" style="min-width:0;">{{ bar.title }}</span>
          </div>
          {% endif %}
        {% endfor %}
      </div>
    {% endif %}

    <div class="grid gap--none h--[{{ grid_pct }}cqh] shrink-0" style="flex-grow:0;height:{{ grid_pct }}cqh;min-height:{{ grid_pct }}cqh;">
      <div class="col--span-1 flex flex--col gap--none">
        {% for row in hour_rows %}
          <!-- Zebra-stripe by hour instead of drawing boundary lines: thin-line techniques
               rendered fine locally but never once showed up on the actual device. A per-hour
               background shade is a large-area fill instead of a 1-2px sliver. -->
          <div class="h--[{{ row.pct }}cqh] w--full flex flex--row cal-row cal-fit-box relative {% if row.shade == 1 %}bg--gray-65{% endif %}" style="justify-content:flex-end;padding-right:8px;">
            <!-- Row height varies a lot with the configured hour range/view size, so this reuses
                 the same fit-text sizing as event titles rather than a hand-tuned fixed size. No
                 minute suffix on any row any more (removed). row.period (only ever set in 12h
                 mode — see run() in transform.js) still shows, but BEFORE the hour digit now,
                 not after: the whole row is right-aligned (justify-content:flex-end +
                 padding-right, not a framework flex--end class — see the flex--center-x/-y note
                 elsewhere in this file on why an explicit style beats a utility class here), and
                 the hour span is the row's LAST flex child — flex-end always packs the row's
                 children flush against its own right edge, so the hour digit's own right edge
                 stays put regardless of whether a period happens to precede it that row, instead
                 of the digit itself shifting per-row the way it would if period trailed after
                 (the exact alignment bug a fixed-width monospace hour span exists to prevent —
                 see below). font-family:monospace + a fixed-width span for just the hour digits
                 keeps every row's ones-place lined up vertically: centering plain "7"/"20" text
                 directly (proportional font, variable width) put each row's OWN glyphs at a
                 different on-screen position for a 1-digit vs 2-digit hour — no framework class
                 reaches "fixed-width monospace digit column" so this is real CSS, not a utility
                 swap. cal-axis-period's own font-size is set in run() as a fraction of its
                 sibling hour span's real computed size (CSS alone can't express "a fraction of a
                 DIFFERENT element's dynamically-fitted size"). -->
              {% if row.period %}<span class="cal-axis-period">{{ row.period }}</span>{% endif %}<span class="label {{ axis_cls }} {% if row.bold %}font--bold {% endif %}cal-fit-text cal-axis-text{% if row.important %} cal-axis-important{% endif %}" data-fit-min="8" data-fit-max="40" style="font-family:monospace;display:inline-block;width:2ch;text-align:right;">{{ row.hour }}</span>
          </div>
        {% endfor %}
      </div>
      {% for day in days limit: shown_days_n %}
        <div class="col--span-{{ day_span }} flex flex--col gap--none border--v-1 relative">
          <!-- Background bands always render at full per-hour granularity even where an event
               is running — events are a separate absolutely-positioned overlay (below), painted
               on top, instead of the background flattening into one shade for the whole span. -->
          {% for seg in day.segments %}
            {% if seg.night %}
              <!-- Sunrise/sunset used to draw a thin tinted line, unreadable on real e-ink at
                   1-3px. Shading the whole night span is a large-area signal instead, same fix
                   as the hour zebra-stripe. -->
              {% assign seg_bg = "bg--gray-30" %}
              {% if seg.shade == 1 %}{% assign seg_bg = "bg--gray-40" %}{% endif %}
            {% elsif day.is_today and seg.past %}
              <!-- An hour today that's already passed reads as "done" — same treatment as a
                   non-today day below. Only upcoming hours keep today's brighter scheme, so the
                   grid visually "fills in" as the day goes on. -->
              {% assign seg_bg = "bg--gray-70" %}
              {% if seg.shade == 1 %}{% assign seg_bg = "bg--gray-55" %}{% endif %}
            {% elsif day.is_today %}
              {% assign seg_bg = "" %}
              {% if seg.shade == 1 %}{% assign seg_bg = "bg--gray-65" %}{% endif %}
            {% else %}
              {% assign seg_bg = "bg--gray-70" %}
              {% if seg.shade == 1 %}{% assign seg_bg = "bg--gray-55" %}{% endif %}
            {% endif %}
            <div class="h--[{{ seg.pct }}cqh] w--full cal-row {{ seg_bg }} relative">
              {% if seg.weather %}
                <!-- Significant weather (rain/snow/storm/fog) as a diagonal hatch — a repeating
                     pattern is still a large-area signal but reads as texture layered on top of
                     the night/day fill, so that stays visible underneath. One treatment for
                     every category for now. -->
                <div class="absolute" style="top:0;left:0;right:0;bottom:0;background-image:repeating-linear-gradient(135deg, rgba(0,0,0,0.4) 0px, rgba(0,0,0,0.4) 3px, transparent 3px, transparent 9px);"></div>
              {% endif %}
            </div>
          {% endfor %}
          {% if day.now_marker %}
            <!-- Independent of the background segment list (it used to be spliced into one and
                 forced to a minimum height by borrowing pct from today's largest segment, which
                 threw off today's zebra-stripe alignment against every other column). -->
            <div class="absolute" style="left:0;right:0;top:{{ day.now_marker.top_pct }}%;">
              {% if day.now_marker.night %}
                <div class="h--[5px] w--full bg--white"></div>
              {% else %}
                <div class="h--[5px] w--full bg--red"></div>
              {% endif %}
            </div>
          {% endif %}
          {% for event in day.events %}
            <!-- Absolutely positioned over the background stack above, sized in plain CSS %
                 (relative to this day column, the nearest position:relative ancestor) rather
                 than cqh — cqh is relative to the whole .layout, which would double-count the
                 header/all-day rows' own height here. Each overlapping event is its own
                 independently-positioned box (top_pct/height_pct are THIS event's real
                 start/end, not a shared cluster span). lane_index/nlanes place it in its
                 horizontal slot among however many events actually overlap it. -->
            <div class="absolute" style="top:{{ event.top_pct }}%;height:{{ event.height_pct }}%;left:calc({{ event.lane_index }} * 100% / {{ event.nlanes }});width:calc(100% / {{ event.nlanes }});box-sizing:border-box;padding:0 2px;">
              <!-- event.hue/event.fg are computed server-side, same as a.hue/a.fg above.
                   text--{{ event.fg }} on this outer div (not just the title span) so the
                   currentColor border inherits it, giving a visible edge even against an
                   equally light/dark background band. NOT cal-row: that class also carries a
                   blanket JS-forced overflow:hidden (needed for content-driven axis/background
                   rows) which would re-clip a too-tall title right after fit() sets this event's
                   own cal-fit-box to overflow:visible. size_class "h--full w--full": this box is
                   sized entirely by its absolutely-positioned ancestor above, not a normal-flow
                   row budget — that's also why fit_min=11/clip=false here (vs. the all-day
                   call's 9/true): fit() can safely leave an oversized title visible-overflow on
                   this overlay. -->
              {% render "chip", hue: event.hue, fg: event.fg, title: event.title, flex_dir: "flex--col", size_class: "h--full w--full", size_style: "", pad_cls: event_pad_cls, box_radius: "7px", border_left: true, border_right: true, margin_left: false, margin_right: false, title_cls: event_title_cls, fit_min: 11, clip: false, pad_right: pad_right %}
            </div>
          {% endfor %}
        </div>
      {% endfor %}
    </div>

    <!-- Footer: daily weather (icon + high/low), its own black zone below the grid — pulled out
         of the header (which used to squeeze this in as a second line) so the header stays one
         line and the weather gets a deliberate zone of its own. Same col--span-1 gutter +
         col--span-{{ day_span }} shape as the header, so icon/temp under each day line up with
         that day's own column. -->
    <div class="grid gap--none h--[{{ footer_pct }}cqh] bg--black shrink-0" style="flex-grow:0;height:{{ footer_pct }}cqh;min-height:{{ footer_pct }}cqh;overflow:hidden;">
      <div class="col--span-1"></div>
      {% for day in days limit: shown_days_n %}
        <div class="col--span-{{ day_span }} flex flex--row flex--center-x flex--center-y gap--xsmall border--v-1" style="overflow:hidden;">
          {% if day.icon %}
            <!-- height:1.6em ties the icon to chip_title_cls's font-size (grows/shrinks across
                 view tiers) while rendering noticeably larger than the text. filter:invert(1):
                 plain black line art would otherwise vanish against this footer's black fill. -->
            <img class="{{ chip_title_cls }} shrink-0" style="height:1.6em;width:auto;filter:invert(1);" src="{{ day.icon }}">
            <!-- color:white inline, not text--white: .label sets its own explicit color, so it
                 wouldn't inherit an ancestor's. Full view gets the spelled-out "Max 27°C Min
                 18°C" (room to spare); every other tier keeps the terser "27° / 18°". Max/Min
                 labels in muted gray so the numbers stay the visually louder white. -->
            {%- if view == 1 -%}
              <!-- .label is display:inline-flex, which discards whitespace-only text nodes
                   between children instead of rendering a gap — margin-right on each piece
                   instead of a plain space. Small inline up/down triangle in place of "Max"/
                   "Min" text: same idea (a quiet marker ahead of the number) in less width, and
                   reads as a real high/low indicator rather than an abbreviation to parse.
                   fill="#999" directly since these sit in a flex row of their own color, not
                   inheriting white from the outer .label the way plain text does. -->
              <span class="label {{ chip_title_cls }}" style="white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;min-width:0;color:white;"><svg viewBox="0 0 10 10" style="width:0.6em;height:0.6em;flex-shrink:0;margin-right:0.2em;" fill="#999"><polygon points="5,1 9,8 1,8"/></svg><span style="margin-right:0.6em;">{{ day.temp.high }}°{{ temp_unit }}</span><svg viewBox="0 0 10 10" style="width:0.6em;height:0.6em;flex-shrink:0;margin-right:0.2em;" fill="#999"><polygon points="5,9 1,2 9,2"/></svg><span>{{ day.temp.low }}°{{ temp_unit }}</span></span>
            {%- else -%}
              <span class="label {{ chip_title_cls }}" style="white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;min-width:0;color:white;">{{ day.temp.high }}° / {{ day.temp.low }}°</span>
            {%- endif -%}
          {% endif %}
        </div>
      {% endfor %}
    </div>
  {% endif %}
</div>

<script>
(function () {
  // Binary-search the largest font size that fits each event title inside its own block,
  // measured against the real rendered box — more robust than guessing a size tier
  // server-side, since it reacts to the block's actual pixel height on whatever renders it.
  // Returns the font size (px) it settled on, or null if there was no box to fit against.
  // Pass `forced` to skip the search and just apply that exact size (used to make a whole
  // group — the hour axis — share one uniform size; see run()).
  function fit(el, forced) {
    var box = el.closest('.cal-fit-box');
    if (!box) return null;
    // A very short event box (many overlapping lanes AND a brief duration, e.g. a 30-minute
    // "Assembly" squeezed into a 3-lane split) can have its own fixed vertical padding
    // (event_pad_cls, a per-view constant with no idea how short THIS SPECIFIC box ended up)
    // alone add up to MORE than the box's whole content height — box.clientHeight already
    // INCLUDES padding (it's fixed by the box's own top/height% positioning either way, padding
    // can't change it), so the space actually left for children is
    // box.clientHeight - padTop - padBottom, which this same padding can push negative. Shrink
    // padding-top/padding-bottom first, proportionally, whenever that's happening, reclaiming
    // real room.
    var boxCsForPad = getComputedStyle(box);
    var padTop = parseFloat(boxCsForPad.paddingTop) || 0;
    var padBottom = parseFloat(boxCsForPad.paddingBottom) || 0;
    var minContentH = 14; // roughly one line of floor-size text
    var contentH = box.clientHeight - padTop - padBottom;
    if (contentH < minContentH && (padTop > 0 || padBottom > 0)) {
      var totalPad = padTop + padBottom;
      var shrink = Math.min(totalPad, minContentH - contentH);
      padTop = Math.max(0, padTop - shrink * (padTop / totalPad));
      padBottom = Math.max(0, padBottom - shrink * (padBottom / totalPad));
      box.style.paddingTop = padTop + 'px';
      box.style.paddingBottom = padBottom + 'px';
      contentH = box.clientHeight - padTop - padBottom;
    }
    // cal-axis-text/cal-fit-clip need real clipping: a normal-flow box that contributes real
    // height to its row's own budget (the hour axis, an all-day chip title) would otherwise
    // auto-expand and misalign the rest of the grid if left too tall. An event title has no
    // such risk — its box is an absolutely-positioned overlay with a fixed top/height
    // regardless of content — so it's allowed to spill past its pill instead (matching
    // TRMNL's own official calendar plugin: fixed size, overflow visible).
    var mustClip = el.classList.contains('cal-axis-text') || el.classList.contains('cal-fit-clip');
    box.style.overflow = mustClip ? 'hidden' : 'visible';
    el.style.display = 'block';         // make width/maxWidth apply predictably
    var min = parseInt(el.getAttribute('data-fit-min'), 10) || 11;
    var max = parseInt(el.getAttribute('data-fit-max'), 10) || 90;

    function setSize(px) {
      el.style.fontSize = px + 'px';
      el.style.lineHeight = Math.round(px * 1.15) + 'px';
    }

    if (forced != null) {
      setSize(forced);
      return forced;
    }

    // clientHeight/clientWidth include the box's own padding, which text can't actually
    // occupy — read the real computed padding rather than guessing a fixed buffer.
    var boxCs = getComputedStyle(box);
    var padX = (parseFloat(boxCs.paddingLeft) || 0) + (parseFloat(boxCs.paddingRight) || 0);
    var availW = box.clientWidth - padX - 2;
    if (availW <= 0) { setSize(min); return min; }
    // Give the element its own hard width ceiling and compare it against ITSELF (scrollWidth
    // vs its own clientWidth) rather than a separate ancestor's measurements — cross-element
    // comparison proved unreliable here.
    el.style.maxWidth = availW + 'px';

    // fitsOneLine: genuinely one line, no wrap at all. fitsWrapped: fits within the box's own
    // real available height, whatever that takes (could be several wrapped lines). Both compare
    // EL's own scrollHeight against `contentH` (computed above: box.clientHeight minus the
    // box's own real padding) — NOT box.clientHeight directly, which INCLUDES that padding and
    // so overstates how much room text actually has by exactly the padding amount (confirmed:
    // "Grocery Shopping" measured as fitting one line against clientHeight alone, then had its
    // real bottom few pixels silently eaten by the padding it never accounted for). Also never
    // box.scrollHeight: an event title's box is deliberately left overflow:visible (see
    // mustClip below, a MODESTLY oversized title is allowed to spill past its pill and stay
    // readable), and a visible-overflow element's scrollHeight doesn't grow to reflect a child
    // overflowing it (confirmed in Chrome) — comparing box.scrollHeight to box.clientHeight was
    // really just comparing box.clientHeight to itself, always true no matter how enormously el
    // had actually overflowed. That let a tall-but-narrow box (many overlapping lanes AND a long
    // duration, e.g. an 8-hour "Work" block split into 2 lanes) accept a font size so huge
    // "Work" wrapped into 4 near-empty lines ("W"/"o"/"r"/"k") and spilled ~170px past its own
    // box — only a single WRAPPED CHARACTER's own sideways width ever stopped the search from
    // climbing further, nothing ever stopped it vertically. The largest one-line size and the
    // largest whole-word-wrapped size are searched independently below and the bigger one
    // wins — never an unbounded character-by-character wrap search.
    function fitsOneLine(px) {
      return el.scrollWidth <= el.clientWidth + 1 && el.scrollHeight <= Math.round(px * 1.15) + 2 && el.scrollHeight <= contentH + 1;
    }
    function fitsWrapped() {
      return el.scrollHeight <= contentH + 1 && el.scrollWidth <= el.clientWidth + 1;
    }

    setSize(max);
    if (fitsOneLine(max)) return max; // already at the ceiling — wrapping could never beat it
    var isMultiWord = /\s/.test(el.textContent);
    // Both searches below range all the way down to absMin (well below the configured floor,
    // `min`) rather than stopping at `min` — a title can be genuinely too WIDE for one line
    // and, independently, its box genuinely too SHORT to wrap even at the floor size (a tight
    // 1-hour slot: two lines need roughly double one line's height, and a floor-size line
    // alone can already exceed a short box). Searching only down to `min` used to fall through
    // to a crude last-resort fallback in exactly that case — always the same tiny floor size,
    // one line, wrap-anywhere — instead of ever trying a still-smaller size that might
    // actually fit cleanly (one line OR whole-word-wrapped). Extending both searches this far
    // is always safe: a binary search for the LARGEST fitting size finds the same answer
    // whether the range below it is searched or not, so ordinary boxes (where `min` already
    // fits) are completely unaffected — this only ever helps the tight cases.
    var absMin = 4;

    // Binary-searches [lo, hi] for the largest size where `test(px)` holds, assuming test is
    // monotonic (true below some threshold, false above it) — same assumption the original
    // single-range search already made. Returns null if not even `lo` passes.
    function searchMax(lo, hi, test) {
      setSize(lo);
      if (!test(lo)) return null;
      while (hi - lo > 1) {
        var mid = Math.floor((lo + hi) / 2);
        setSize(mid);
        if (test(mid)) { lo = mid; } else { hi = mid; }
      }
      return lo;
    }

    var oneLineSize = searchMax(absMin, max, fitsOneLine);

    // A multi-word title may fit a LARGER size wrapped across 2-3 lines than squeezed onto
    // one — "Sports Day Mia" in a tall 2-hour block shouldn't shrink to fit one line when the
    // box has room to spare stacked. Searched with overflowWrap:'normal' (word boundaries
    // only, never mid-word) specifically so this can never repeat the one-word "Work"
    // disaster below: a single word has no boundary to break at, so fitsWrapped's width check
    // (scrollWidth <= clientWidth) still fails past that word's own natural one-line width no
    // matter how tall the box is — bounded exactly like the one-line search above, just now
    // allowed to stack whole words onto more lines instead of only checking the first. A
    // single-word title (isMultiWord false) skips this entirely and always keeps its one-line
    // result, so this can only ever help, never regress, the one-word case — a single word
    // NEVER wraps or splits mid-word (see the "Work" -> "Wor"/"k" incident), full stop.
    var wrappedSize = null;
    if (isMultiWord) {
      el.style.overflowWrap = 'normal';
      wrappedSize = searchMax(absMin, max, fitsWrapped);
    }

    if (oneLineSize !== null && (wrappedSize === null || wrappedSize <= oneLineSize)) {
      el.style.overflowWrap = 'anywhere';
      setSize(oneLineSize);
      return oneLineSize;
    }
    if (wrappedSize !== null) {
      el.style.overflowWrap = 'normal';
      setSize(wrappedSize);
      return wrappedSize;
    }

    // Neither a whole one-line render nor whole-word wrapping fits even at absMin — a
    // pathologically small box (narrower than a single glyph at the smallest legible size).
    // Clip rather than ever break a word apart, single- or multi-word alike.
    el.style.overflowWrap = isMultiWord ? 'normal' : 'anywhere';
    setSize(absMin);
    box.style.overflow = 'hidden';
    return absMin;
  }

  function run() {
    var root = document.getElementById('cal-root');
    if (!root) return;
    // Every h--[Ncqh] row must be prevented from growing past its allocation — a flex/grid
    // child otherwise auto-expands to fit oversized content, cascading into the whole grid
    // overflowing. Constrain first, then fit.
    var rows = root.querySelectorAll('.cal-row');
    for (var r = 0; r < rows.length; r++) rows[r].style.overflow = 'hidden';

    // Rotated to portrait, the full-view "OG" device has much taller hour rows to spend (same
    // total pixels, redistributed from width to height) — raise the fit-text ceiling for
    // events and the hour axis there. Only raises the ceiling; the binary search still only
    // picks a bigger size if the real box can fit it.
    var lgPortrait = !!document.querySelector('.screen--lg.screen--portrait');

    // Event titles each fit independently — different lengths, different lane widths.
    var titles = root.querySelectorAll('.cal-fit-text:not(.cal-axis-text)');
    for (var i = 0; i < titles.length; i++) {
      if (lgPortrait) titles[i].setAttribute('data-fit-max', '100');
      fit(titles[i]);
    }

    // Hour axis numbers share ONE size within their own group instead of fitting
    // independently: "10"/"23" are wider than "0"-"9", so independent fitting left two-digit
    // hours visibly smaller than the one-digit hours beside them. Fit each once, then force
    // every one in the group down to the smallest of those ceilings.
    //
    // Two separate groups (important-range hours vs. the compressed hours outside it) rather
    // than one group across all 24 — a single extremely thin compressed row can't legibly fit
    // any size regardless, and sharing a minimum would tank the readable important-range
    // labels down to that same tiny floor.
    function fitAxisGroup(selector, maxOverride) {
      var els = root.querySelectorAll(selector);
      var minSize = null;
      for (var j = 0; j < els.length; j++) {
        if (maxOverride) els[j].setAttribute('data-fit-max', maxOverride);
        var s = fit(els[j]);
        if (s != null && (minSize === null || s < minSize)) minSize = s;
      }
      if (minSize !== null) {
        for (var k = 0; k < els.length; k++) fit(els[k], minSize);
      }
    }
    fitAxisGroup('.cal-axis-important', lgPortrait ? '48' : null);
    fitAxisGroup('.cal-axis-text:not(.cal-axis-important)', lgPortrait ? '48' : null);

    // AM/PM (12h mode only — see row.period in transform.js) is a normal flex sibling now, not
    // absolutely positioned (no per-row placement math needed once it's just another child of
    // the same flex-end row) — only its font-size can't come from CSS alone, since it has to
    // track its sibling hour span's own real FITTED size, which varies per axis group above.
    var periodEls = root.querySelectorAll('.cal-axis-period');
    for (var p = 0; p < periodEls.length; p++) {
      var periodEl = periodEls[p];
      var hourEl = periodEl.nextElementSibling;
      if (!hourEl) continue;
      var hourPx = parseFloat(hourEl.style.fontSize) || 16;
      periodEl.style.fontSize = Math.round(hourPx * 0.72) + 'px';
    }
  }

  // Wait for the Inter webfont to finish loading before measuring — if fit() measures against
  // the fallback font first, the later swap reflows text past a box it was already fitted to,
  // with nothing left to react. But document.fonts.ready can be slow (or never resolve on a
  // render sandbox with restricted font fetches), and never running fit() at all is worse than
  // running it once against the fallback. Race a hard timeout against fonts.ready so fit()
  // always runs at least once quickly, then again (idempotent) once fonts.ready resolves.
  //
  // The FIRST call used to only ever happen via setTimeout(400)/fonts.ready — both always at
  // least one tick away, never synchronous — so a render pipeline that screenshots right after
  // DOMContentLoaded/'load' (TRMNL's own device screenshot, some headless tools) could capture
  // the page with EVERY title still at its raw, un-fitted CSS default size: exactly what a
  // single word breaking mid-word looks like (a large default size on a box too narrow for it,
  // wrapping — with no explicit overflow-wrap set yet either way — at whatever break the
  // browser's own default text layout allows). Calling run() synchronously here too closes
  // that gap: by the time this function returns, at least one real fit pass has already
  // happened against whatever font is available RIGHT NOW, before any external tool gets a
  // chance to screenshot. The later calls still matter (this first pass may be against a
  // fallback font, or an even-earlier viewport/layout state) but are no longer the ONLY chance.
  function runAfterFonts() {
    run();
    setTimeout(run, 400);
    if (document.fonts && document.fonts.ready) {
      document.fonts.ready.then(run).catch(run);
    }
  }

  if (document.readyState === 'loading') {
    document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', runAfterFonts);
  } else {
    runAfterFonts();
  }
})();
</script>
{% endtemplate %}
